Vita: textures of earlier stages are released from the 32 MiB GXM texture pool.

Aurora keeps every converted texture alive in its content cache up to 128 MiB, sized for desktop
VRAM. On the Vita the GXM pool is 32 MiB, so after a few stage changes the pool stayed full of
earlier stages' textures (only the cache held them) and new ones failed to allocate: black
textures (Vita3K soak: gxm_texture_alloc_failures 10,104 by 405 s; every tour run since 100-200 s).
On the Vita the content cache keeps 8 MiB, and release_idle() lets the platform drop the
object-cache entries not bound in the last frames plus the content cache when a GPU texture
allocation fails, then retry it. Textures in use stay; nothing else changes.

--- a/lib/gx/texture.hpp
+++ b/lib/gx/texture.hpp
@@ -24,7 +24,12 @@
 const TextureStats& texture_stats() noexcept;
 
 namespace texture {
+#if defined(__vita__)
+// Vita: textures live in a 32 MiB GXM pool, so the content cache keeps a small reuse window.
+constexpr uint64_t ContentCacheBudgetBytes = 8ull * 1024ull * 1024ull;
+#else
 constexpr uint64_t ContentCacheBudgetBytes = 128ull * 1024ull * 1024ull;
+#endif
 constexpr uint64_t ObjectCacheIdleFrames = 600;
 constexpr bool AsyncTextureReplacements = true;
 constexpr uint32_t ReplacementThumbnailDim = 64;
@@ -39,6 +44,12 @@
 void end_frame() noexcept;
 void shutdown() noexcept;
 void set_content_cache_budget_for_testing(uint64_t bytes) noexcept;
+#if defined(__vita__)
+// GPU texture memory pressure (Vita): drops the object-cache entries not bound in the last
+// idleFrames frames and the whole content cache, so textures only these caches still hold
+// (earlier stages) are destroyed and their pool memory returns. Bound textures stay.
+void release_idle(uint64_t idleFrames) noexcept;
+#endif
 
 // Internal resolvers exposed for cache tests.
 gfx::TextureHandle resolve_static_texture(const GXTexObj_& obj);
